summary:
SL-039 holds up on real data from this PC as well as on the test trees. I did a fresh single-file self-contained publish (a 139.8 MB exe). Then I scanned two real system folders, C:\Windows and C:\ProgramData, in two ways: through the public Core API with a scratch probe that caught every published snapshot, and through the shipped exe.

C:\Windows has 346,873 entries: 47,409 later hardlink names sitting in a different row from the name that was charged, 52 unreadable folders (7 of them top-level rows), and 223 files that take less space on disk than their size. C:\ProgramData has 257,972 entries: 5 top-level junctions, 26,673 files that take less space on disk than their size, and 9,013 paths over 260 characters. On both:
- every captured snapshot's rows added up to that snapshot's totals (58/58, 17/17, 31/31 and 101/101 across the runs);
- no row's state ever moved backwards;
- no Done or Unreadable row changed files, folders or unreadable-below later;
- every final row equalled the store's own rollup of that row's subtree (126/126 and 58/58);
- ScanResult.TopLevel was the same object as the last progress snapshot.

On real data, one C:\Windows row changed its bytes after it was Done. That is the documented hardlink first-name correction.

In the shipped exe on C:\Windows, the hook's watch mode found the list mid-scan with 126 rows: one folder row already at 7.4 GB and 48 rows not read yet, with Cancel pressable. After Cancel, the report's topLevel matched core exactly and was final, with no row left reading. The completed-scan reports for both folders matched core too. Across 184 top-level names, none appeared in any report. Real rows run through the app's row converter read as intended:
- 'Microsoft  (stopped partway) | 11.5 GB (6.0 GB on disk) … 75 folders inside could not be opened'
- 'Prefetch  (could not be opened)'
- 'Desktop  (link, not followed) | 0 bytes'

The project's own suites also pass:
- build: 0 warnings, 0 errors;
- Core tests: 170/170;
- window cases: all 18 default cases, including L-001, L-002 and L-003;
- HookCases: H-001 and H-002;
- M-001 at 100,000 rows: refresh p95 67.9 ms (under the 100 ms limit, but a narrower margin than the build's 25–46 ms), Input p95 6.8 ms, 5 containers;
- Fitness: all 28 rules hold.

Not reached:
- Criterion 10's 'scan faults after rows were shown' sub-shape. There is no seam in the shipped exe that can make the scan throw, so only the code path was read.
- The stale network pass. It is the person's run, because it changes SMB mappings.
- A whole big drive. The largest scan was 347k entries.

Two more things stay open. The person has not yet said whether a one-level list closes PS-002's 'results fill in as it goes'. And another session (SL-040) was running its own window cases at the same time on this machine: the lingering FsAnalyser.App process and the new %TEMP%\fsa-window-cases folder belong to it and were left untouched. The same overlap may have pushed up this run's M-001 timings.

